%[text] # **Retrieval Model and Eigenvalue Analysis Data**
%[text] The analysis itself is intentionally simple. The purpose is to inspect how OpenSeesMatlab returns node, element, modal, and eigenvector data in forms suitable for later MATLAB processing.
clc; clear; close all;
%[text] ### Model Data
opsMAT = OpenSeesMatlab();
ops = opsMAT.opensees;
%[text] For example, the `tool` property provides a function `loadExamples` to run some built-in models. Of course, you can run your own model; the built-in model is used here for demonstration purposes only.
opsMAT.utils.loadExamples("Frame3D");
% or your model here
%[text] We can visualize the model using the `plotModel` function in the `vis` attribute.
figure;
h = opsMAT.vis.plotModel(); %[output:9182737b] %[output:21290799]
%[text] We can retrieve data from the current model, which returns a nested `struct`. You can view the data using MATLAB's workspace variables.
modelData = opsMAT.post.getModelData();
disp(modelData) %[output:26ad7be2]
%[text] Finally, we can print the information for each field.
S = modelData;

stack = {{'S', S}};

while ~isempty(stack) %[output:group:50b5e478]
    item = stack{end};
    stack(end) = [];

    name = item{1};
    val  = item{2};

    if isstruct(val)
        fns = fieldnames(val);
        for i = numel(fns):-1:1
            f = fns{i};
            stack{end+1} = {sprintf('%s.%s', name, f), val.(f)};
        end
    else
        sz = strjoin(string(size(val)), 'x');
        fprintf('%s : %s\n', name, sz); %[output:34e69e1a]
    end
end %[output:group:50b5e478]
%[text] ### Eigen Data
%[text] First, we need to save the eigenvalue analysis results data for future reuse.
tag = 1;
opsMAT.post.saveEigenData(tag, 10, solver='-genBandArpack'); %[output:7d373cbb]
%[text] Then, data is retrieved from the file, returning a nested `structure` that stores the various results of the eigenvalue analysis.
eigenData = opsMAT.post.getEigenData(odbTag=tag);
disp(eigenData) %[output:99a5cdac]
%[text] Using this data, we can visualize the first modal shapes.
figure;
opsMAT.vis.plotEigen(6, eigenData); %[output:8c9b324e]
axis off %[output:8c9b324e]
S = eigenData;

stack = {{'S', S}};

while ~isempty(stack) %[output:group:555d01a0]
    item = stack{end};
    stack(end) = [];

    name = item{1};
    val  = item{2};

    if isstruct(val)
        fns = fieldnames(val);
        for i = numel(fns):-1:1
            f = fns{i};
            stack{end+1} = {sprintf('%s.%s', name, f), val.(f)};
        end
    else
        sz = strjoin(string(size(val)), 'x');
        fprintf('%s : %s\n', name, sz); %[output:51f59bea]
    end
end %[output:group:555d01a0]
%[text] Let's look at the period of each mode.
S.ModalProps.raw.eigenPeriod %[output:0dfe4303]
%[text] Let's look at the **cumulative participation mass ratio (%)** in each direction.
S.ModalProps.raw.partiMassRatiosCumuMX %[output:90228d22]
S.ModalProps.raw.partiMassRatiosCumuMY %[output:48ebbaf8]
S.ModalProps.raw.partiMassRatiosCumuMZ %[output:6e412e7c]
S.ModalProps.raw.partiMassRatiosCumuRMX %[output:73400a51]
S.ModalProps.raw.partiMassRatiosCumuRMY %[output:2ec209e5]
S.ModalProps.raw.partiMassRatiosCumuRMZ %[output:25766e7a]
%[text] ### Checking returned data
%[text] Verify tag ordering and array dimensions before indexing returned model data. Eigenvectors are organized by node, degree of freedom, and mode; a plotted mode shape is a useful ordering check.

